1
00:00:00,233 --> 00:00:03,062
A blood-delivery drone saved 79 minutes.

2
00:00:03,251 --> 00:00:04,430
Or was it just 19?

3
00:00:04,631 --> 00:00:06,376
Both numbers come from the same study.

4
00:00:06,788 --> 00:00:11,389
In Rwanda, a peer-reviewed study covered 12,733

5
00:00:11,399 --> 00:00:13,863
drone blood orders to 20 hospitals.

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:14,928
The drone:

7
00:00:15,106 --> 00:00:16,933
a median of 41 minutes.

8
00:00:17,134 --> 00:00:18,549
The hospital's ambulance:

9
00:00:18,573 --> 00:00:20,791
120, because it had to drive there

10
00:00:20,801 --> 00:00:21,532
and back.

11
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:22,322
The gap:

12
00:00:22,358 --> 00:00:23,159
79.

13
00:00:23,584 --> 00:00:25,355
But against the best case, a car

14
00:00:25,365 --> 00:00:27,348
that only goes one way, about an

15
00:00:27,358 --> 00:00:29,409
hour, the gap shrinks to 19.

16
00:00:29,804 --> 00:00:30,960
It's like saying you're tall.

17
00:00:31,149 --> 00:00:31,879
Next to whom?

18
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:35,030
And 67% less expired blood at 12

19
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:35,700
months.

20
00:00:35,901 --> 00:00:36,761
The fine print:

21
00:00:36,856 --> 00:00:39,688
no control group, estimated road times, and

22
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:41,018
no cost per delivery.

23
00:00:41,428 --> 00:00:43,303
So when you see a number, ask:

24
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:44,187
compared to what?

25
00:00:44,376 --> 00:00:46,277
And check its evidence level, from A

26
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:46,864
to D.

27
00:00:47,265 --> 00:00:49,177
A number without its comparison is half

28
00:00:49,187 --> 00:00:49,823
a number.

29
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,732
Save it, and share it with someone

30
00:00:51,742 --> 00:00:53,262
who believes any number.

